## Why This Is Asked
This tests ability to design a lightweight, repeatable prompt-testing workflow and catch prompt drift early.

## Key Concepts
- Prompt versioning
- Drift metrics (embedding similarity, content guards)
- Reproducibility and logging

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal prototype skeleton for drift check
function driftFlag(basePrompt, paraphrase) {
  const baseOut = model(basePrompt)
  const paraOut = model(paraphrase)
  const sim = cosineSimilarity(embed(baseOut), embed(paraOut))
  return sim < 0.8
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this for multi-language prompts?
- How would you integrate with CI to prevent drift before deploy?

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes ability to design a practical, testable clarifier routing mechanism, not just theory.

## Key Concepts
- Rule-based routing and simple scoring
- Signals: intent_confidence, data_availability, user_role
- Edge-case handling: ambiguity, conflicts, missing data
- Payload design for downstream modules

## Code Example
```javascript
// Tiny prototype
function chooseClarifier(signals){
  const {intent_confidence, data_availability, user_role} = signals;
  if (intent_confidence < 0.5) return 'request-region';
  if (data_availability.includes('period')) return 'request-period';
  if (user_role==='analytics') return 'request-metric';
  return 'request-period';
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this for multilingual prompts?
- How would you evaluate clarifier quality in automated tests?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a modular, policy-driven prompt pipeline with per-tenant governance, low-latency guarantees, and auditable outputs. It also probes debugging, latency budgeting, and guard interactions.

## Key Concepts

- Policy-driven routing
- Guard modules and composition
- Per-tenant audits and latency accounting
- Resilience to module failures

## Code Example

```python
import time
from typing import List, Dict

def select_modules(risk: str) -> List[str]:
    if risk == "high":
        return ["RedactionGuard","PrivacyGuard"]
    elif risk == "medium":
        return ["PrivacyGuard","LatencyGuard"]
    else:
        return ["LatencyGuard"]

def apply_guards(p: str, modules: List[str]) -> str:
    s = p
    for m in modules:
        if m == "RedactionGuard":
            s = s.replace("SSN", "[REDACTED]")
        if m == "PrivacyGuard":
            s = s.replace("email", "em[REDACTED]")
        if m == "LatencyGuard":
            time.sleep(0.01)  # simulate guard overhead
    return s

def audit(p: str, g: str, modules: List[str], latency: float) -> Dict:
    return {"input": p, "guarded": g, "modules": modules, "latency_ms": int(latency*1000)}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle policy drift and tenant onboarding?
- How would you test for adversarial prompts attempting to bypass guards?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to design modular, policy-driven prompt systems that scale across tenants, balance latency with safety, and provide auditable provenance.

## Key Concepts

- Modular prompt blocks
- Policy graph
- Per-tenant constraints
- Latency-aware composition
- Determinism and reproducibility
- Provenance and audit

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal prototype sketch (pseudo-implementation)
function composePrompt(intent, blockLibrary, policy){
  const allowed = blockLibrary.filter(b => policy.allows(b.tag, intent));
  const prompt = allowed.map(b => b.template).join("\n");
  const audit = { intent, blocks: allowed.map(b => b.id), provenance: policy.provenance };
  return { prompt, audit };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate determinism when blocks or transformations are nondeterministic?
- How would policy graph evolution affect audit integrity and prompt reproducibility?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design end-to-end prompt orchestration under SLA; covers multi-model choice, caching, and budget-based pruning in a multi-tenant context.

## Key Concepts

- Dynamic plan generation with per-model latency and cost profiles
- Content-addressable caching to reduce repeated work
- Budget-aware pruning and graceful degradation
- Tenant isolation and guardrails in orchestration

## Code Example

```javascript
class MockModel {
  constructor(name, tokenCost, latencyMs){
    this.name = name; this.tokenCost = tokenCost; this.latencyMs = latencyMs;
  }
  run(prompt){
    return new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(() => resolve(`${this.name}-out:${prompt.slice(0,60)}`), this.latencyMs));
  }
}

const modelA = new MockModel('ModelA', 0.5, 120);
const modelB = new MockModel('ModelB', 0.2, 60);
const cache = new Map();
function hash(s){ let h=0; for(let i=0;i<s.length;i++){ h=(h<<5)-h+s.charCodeAt(i); h|=0;} return h.toString(); }
function plan(intent){ return [ [modelA, intent+" [segment1]"], [modelB, intent+" [segment2]"] ]; }
async function runPlan(intent, budgetTokens, maxLatencyMs=300){
  const key = hash(intent);
  if(cache.has(key)) return cache.get(key);
  const steps = plan(intent);
  let totalLatency = 0; const outputs = [];
  for(const [m,p] of steps){ const out = await m.run(p); outputs.push(out); totalLatency += m.latencyMs; if(totalLatency>maxLatencyMs) break; }
  const result = outputs.join(' | ');
  cache.set(key, result);
  return result;
}
```
